{"lines": ["In 1959, two Cornell physicists suggested that any alien civilization might broadcast on 1420 megahertz, the frequency hydrogen naturally gives off.", "In 1973, Ohio State University pointed its Big Ear radio telescope at the sky to listen for exactly that.", "It became the longest-running search of its kind in history.", "On August 15th, 1977, Big Ear recorded something at sixteen minutes past eleven at night.", "Days later, a volunteer named Jerry Ehman was going through the printouts by hand, and a row of characters stopped him.", "The signal was strong, narrow, and appeared to come from the direction of Sagittarius.", "It lasted the full seventy-two seconds that Big Ear could watch it.", "The telescope sampled the sky for ten seconds at a time, then spent two seconds processing, and printed one character for each ten-second average.", "A space meant barely above background noise. The numbers climbed from one to nine, and then the letters began.", "The Wow! signal climbed all the way to U.", "Ehman circled the code \"6EQUJ5\" and wrote one word beside it: \"Wow!\"", "That code is not a message. It only records how strong the signal was, in ten-second steps.", "Scientists have searched that patch of sky again and again.", "The signal has never come back, and no explanation has been confirmed.", "Comets, space debris, and interstellar scintillation were all considered. None of them has been proven.", "But a signal that appears once is one thing. The next one never stops."], "words": [["In", 0.0, 0.08], ["1959,", 0.08, 1.16], ["two", 1.94, 2.22], ["Cornell", 2.22, 2.52], ["physicists", 2.52, 3.06], ["suggested", 3.06, 3.76], ["that", 3.76, 4.14], ["any", 4.14, 4.3], ["alien", 4.3, 4.7], ["civilization", 4.7, 5.2], ["might", 5.2, 6.22], ["broadcast", 6.22, 6.76], ["on", 6.76, 7.14], ["1420", 7.14, 8.68], ["megahertz,", 8.68, 10.22], ["the", 10.22, 10.32], ["frequency", 10.32, 10.66], ["hydrogen", 10.66, 11.32], ["naturally", 11.32, 11.84], ["gives", 11.84